WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.880
Hello there!&nbsp;

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.600
Let’s suppose you are Microsoft and you&nbsp;
are releasing a huge and awesome model.&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.360
How do you make it obvious it is the&nbsp;
best, the biggest, the real thing?

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.920
Correct, you compare your numbers&nbsp;
of parameter against the literature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.440
make a beautiful plot where&nbsp;
you are the top of the line!&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.760
So congrats, now everyone is hailing&nbsp;
you like you’re the best of the best.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.360
And you might as well be, Ms. Coffee&nbsp;
Bean is not here to claim otherwise.&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.760
But what does the number of parameters&nbsp;
tell us about a model anyway?

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.800
We came across a paper that tells us about the&nbsp;
bigger picture: It argues that the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.480
parameters alone is not enough to compare against&nbsp;
other models and that reporting one number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.840
only about a model can be highly misleading&nbsp;
when it comes to describing its efficiency.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.040
In other words, a high number of&nbsp;
parameters does not necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.720
mean that the model has high&nbsp;
capacity, nor that it is slow.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.000
So, get ready to get your mind enlarged&nbsp;
as we welcome you to this AI Coffee Break!

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.640
Thanks to Aleph Alpha for sponsoring this video!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.400
Stick around until the end of the&nbsp;
video to learn more about them.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.720
Model comparisons. What is this all about?&nbsp;
Well, we need to measure scientific progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.240
in deep learning. One line of doing so is&nbsp;
to measure the models quality on specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.760
tasks or benchmarks and to measure how much&nbsp;
of a sensible output the model delivers.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.040
But we do not talk about this today, nor&nbsp;
does this paper. Because today it’s all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:33.040
model efficiency which relates to: how much&nbsp;
training a model costs, how much one has to pay to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:39.360
run inference for many users and how long a user&nbsp;
has to wait until the model delivers the answer.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.120
To describe model efficiency,&nbsp;
the literature uses things like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.640
we cite, “the number of trainable parameters, the&nbsp;
number of floating-point operations (FLOPs), and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:55.680
speed/throughput”. But the point of this paper is,
that if one chooses the “right” cost indicators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:01.760
one can create a more favorable picture for&nbsp;
the model’s efficiency than it is in reality,

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.240
and that model comparisons relying on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.760
only some of these metrics can result&nbsp;
in unfair and incomplete comparisons.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.920
This is what the authors call the&nbsp;
“efficiency misnomer”. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.080
to avoid the current situation where people&nbsp;
are just choosing the metrics they shine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:22.240
at and ignore the others, the&nbsp;
authors propose that… well:&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.960
All metrics should be reported when it&nbsp;
comes to comparing architectures in general.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.680
But what are these metrics and why does&nbsp;
one measure alone not suffice to describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.360
the computational cost of the model? Why&nbsp;
is Ms. Coffee Bean’s secret model of 530B&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.960
and one parameter, not automatically the best of&nbsp;
the best, and also not necessarily the slowest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:49.840
since it reportedly – but don’t fact check&nbsp;
this – has the most parameters in the world?

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.320
Well, let’s look at the efficiency measure of&nbsp;
floating point operations (or FLOPs in short)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.720
that is frequently used to measure&nbsp;
the computational cost in literature.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.680
The more operations you have to do, the more&nbsp;
computations there need to be executed by a piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:11.600
of hardware. But as the authors point out, we&nbsp;
cite, “a model with low FLOPs may not actually be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.948
fast, given that FLOPs does not take into account&nbsp;
information such as degree of parallelism (e.g.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.948 --> 00:03:22.160
depth, recurrence) or hardware-related&nbsp;
details like the cost of a memory access.”

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.840
So supposing that model A has a lower number&nbsp;
FLOPs than model B, it might as well be that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:34.240
model B is more efficient because the floating&nbsp;
point operations are not executed in sequence like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.280
in a Recurrent Neural Network, but in parallel&nbsp;
all at the same time, like in a Transformer.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.520
Or even more, maybe model B is better&nbsp;
suited for the hardware it runs on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:48.400
and for example memory access is&nbsp;
faster for model B than for model A.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.320
So even though model A has lower&nbsp;
FLOPs than B, model B could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.000
much more computationally efficient.
Then, let’s look at the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:01.440
trainable parameters in a model, which is&nbsp;
also a widely used measure for efficiency.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.960
But the authors here draw the attention to the&nbsp;
fact that, very few trainable parameters can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:13.040
still be very slow, especially when the same&nbsp;
parameters are used in the same computation,&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.240
like it is the case with convolutions in CNNs&nbsp;
and with the feed forward layer in transformers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.160
where the same feed forward&nbsp;
network, so the same parameters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.240
are used for each token, as we&nbsp;
discussed in a previous video.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:30.400
So while the number of parameters from the&nbsp;
fully connected layer does not increase with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.040
the sequence length for transformers,&nbsp;
the compute and memory definitely do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:41.200
because all these outputs for each feed forward&nbsp;
layer have to be computed and stored in memory.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.840
The authors highlight that the number of&nbsp;
parameters of a model is a good proxy for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:51.680
knowing whether the model fits in the memory,&nbsp;
but cannot be a cost indicator, or not alone.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.240
Also, the number of parameters is often used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.840
imply the capacity of the model&nbsp;
in the sense of its expressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.640
power of capturing complex functions and&nbsp;
dependencies between input and output.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:07.120
Which is not necessarily true, because&nbsp;
parameter sharing, which reduces the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.320
effective number of parameters, does not&nbsp;
necessarily decrease the model expressivity.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.440
Just think about it:
If we would remove the parameter sharing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.760
in transformers and each token would have its&nbsp;
own feed forward network with its own parameters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.920
which would make the number of parameters&nbsp;
increase linearly with the sequence length,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.560
would that increase the accuracy of the&nbsp;
model and would that model necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.960
learn a better function? No,&nbsp;
it wouldn’t, not necessarily.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:40.960
I mean: we would have N position-specific&nbsp;
feed forward networks instead of just one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:44.560
but it’s questionable that this&nbsp;
would model anything better or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.560
that it would even converge with&nbsp;
the data amounts we have currently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:53.840
because we would have removed an important&nbsp;
inductive bias of the transformer, which is: that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:59.680
every token is treated the same, modulo what the&nbsp;
attention scores dynamically computed beforehand.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:05.440
And yes, it does look like increasing the&nbsp;
number of parameters also increases the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:10.320
accuracy and quality, but this again,&nbsp;
is just an impression we get because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.440
increase of numbers of parameters usually comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.480
with an increase in training data and&nbsp;
with some specific architecture changes.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:24.160
So no, it’s not just about the number of&nbsp;
parameters, but also about the architecture and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:30.400
its inductive biases, the data, again the data&nbsp;
and then all the training gimmicks, of course.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:35.040
Other metrics, like throughput and speed, which&nbsp;
relate to how much a user has to wait for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.360
model to give an answer, strongly&nbsp;
depend on hardware and sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.280
simply on the skill of the programmer who&nbsp;
implemented the whole thing and does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.480
necessarily speak of an architecture on itself.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:51.680
So this measure again, if used on its own,&nbsp;
is not a fair comparison between models.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:57.360
And let’s follow the paper a little closer and&nbsp;
dive into the discrepancy between the training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:01.840
and inference scenarios, that adds a&nbsp;
whole new dimension to the discussion.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:07.120
Depending on context, training, inference or&nbsp;
even both can be more important to look at.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:12.240
For example, Ms. Coffee Bean might not have&nbsp;
the data, the compute, enough money to pay the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:17.680
electricity bill of the TPUs and GPUs that&nbsp;
Microsoft and Google and other likes have,

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:23.840
to train a huge model on billions of data&nbsp;
samples, but she does have enough VRAM to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:29.680
load the model and fine-tune it on more tasks.&nbsp;
This is one of the appealing aspects of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:35.040
pre-train and fine-tune paradigm, that one&nbsp;
general purpose model is trained just once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:41.040
and the environmental damage is done only once,
and this training cost, we cite, “can be very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:46.800
small compared to the inference cost when a model&nbsp;
is deployed to be used by many, many users.”.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.000
But in cases where retraining&nbsp;
has to be done very often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.120
like it is the case with recommender systems&nbsp;
that have to be kept up to date with the user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:02.480
preferences and available content at any moment,&nbsp;
the training cost can become very important again.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:07.760
On the other hand, if the inference efficiency&nbsp;
is the bottleneck because the deep-learning based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:14.320
application runs on mobile phones, then&nbsp;
the inference speed is very important.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:18.960
So we have seen how different measures can&nbsp;
disagree when it comes to describing efficiency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:24.000
for the same model and that one measure of&nbsp;
efficiency alone can be highly misleading.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.160
Also, the number of parameters alone&nbsp;
is not a measure for much else than:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:33.760
how large a storage the model needs and that&nbsp;
the more efficiency metrics a paper reports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:39.120
the better, especially to be sure that nobody&nbsp;
“misses out” on the unfavorable metrics.

